## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives and common OS patterns, especially producer-consumer, deadlock avoidance, and correct semaphore/mutex usage in C.

## Key Concepts
- Semaphores
- Mutex
- Circular buffer
- Deadlock detection
- Boundary conditions (full/empty)

## Code Example
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 10

int buffer[N];
int in = 0, out = 0;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void produce(int item) {
  sem_wait(&empty);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  buffer[in] = item;
  in = (in + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&full);
}

int consume() {
  int item;
  sem_wait(&full);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  item = buffer[out];
  out = (out + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&empty);
  return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers and ensure fairness?
- What are trade-offs vs. using condition variables instead of semaphores?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design scalable I/O budgeting, cross-CPU coordination, and NUMA-aware fairness under heavy multi-tenant workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Per-VM isolation and quotas
- NUMA-aware accounting
- Token-bucket throttling
- Cross-CPU coordination via IPI
- CAS-based atomic updates

## Code Example

```c
struct vm_budget {
    atomic_long budget_bytes;
    atomic_long tokens;
    int numa_node;
};

void refill(struct vm_budget *vb) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&vb->tokens, REFILL_TOKEN);
}

static bool try_consume(struct vm_budget *vb, long cost) {
    long old, new;
    do {
        old = atomic_load(&vb->tokens);
        if (old < cost)
            return false;
        new = old - cost;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&vb->tokens, &old, new));
    return true;
}

void submit_io(struct vm_budget *vb, struct bio *bio) {
    if (!try_consume(vb, bio->size))
        enqueue_throttled(vb->numa_node, bio);
    else
        dispatch_io(bio);
}
```

